#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107119 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives. \n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) & =\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & ={sech}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x)-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "df316238-1ead-50db-80ec-d47656e6acad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107120 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n%$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x}).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." :tutorial/id #uuid "11e38932-d6a4-5649-bbbe-205fb1bad98d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5221 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107121 :tutorial/content "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-1}{{e}^{2x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({e}^{2x}-1)^{′}\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)\\cdot ({e}^{2x}+1)^{′}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2{e}^{2x})({e}^{2x}+1)-({e}^{2x}-1)(2{e}^{2x})}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}-2{e}^{4x}+2{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2x}}{({e}^{2x}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x}+1})}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}})}^{2} \\\\  & ={sech}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7389d5-4ba5-5fd7-8edb-b983483e6099" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5223 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Tangent"}]}
